===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[wind]] or [[twist]] in the manner of a corkscrew; to move with much [[horizontal]] and [[vertical]] shifting.
#* {{quote-text|year=1832|author={{w|Charles Dickens}}|title=[[s:The Pickwick Papers|The Pickwick Papers]]|chapter=35|passage=Into the tea–room Mr. Pickwick turned; and catching sight of him, Mr. Bantam '''corkscrewed''' his way through the crowd and welcomed him with ecstasy.}}
#* {{quote-text|year=1916|author={{w|John Buchan}}|title=[[s:Greenmantle|Greenmantle]]|chapter=10|passage=The street '''corkscrewed''' endlessly. Sometimes it seemed to stop; then it found a hole in the opposing masonry and edged its way in.}}
#* {{quote-text|year=1960|author={{w|Lobsang Rampa}}|title=[[s:The Rampa Story|The Rampa Story]]|chapter=5|passage=Far off to starboard an Atlantic liner, all lights blazing, came towards us, '''corkscrewing''' with a motion which must have left the passengers unhappy.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause something to [[twist]] or move in a [[spiral]] path or shape.
#* {{quote-text|year=1851|author={{w|Herman Melville}}|title=[[s:Moby-Dick|Moby-Dick]]|chapter=134|passage=Caught and twisted—'''corkscrewed''' in the mazes of the line, loose harpoons and lances, with all their bristling barbs and points, came flashing and dripping up to the chocks in the bows of Ahab’s boat.}}
#* {{quote-text|year=2006|author=Rocky Raab|title=Baggy Zero Four|page=155|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=JlB6NZ7EvHQC&pg=PA155|passage=Rusty '''corkscrewed''' the plane back down again, but instead of mashing the throttles to the wall, he pulled them to idle.}}
#* {{quote-text|year=2007|author=Mike Monahan|title=Barracuda|page=107|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=hkKH8IXLkzIC&pg=PA116|passage=Soon he was '''corkscrewed''' into place, suspended from the ceiling in an impossible maze of unforgiving circuitry.<!-- possibly a distinct sense? -->}}
# To [[extract]] [[information]] or [[consent]] from someone.
#* {{quote-text|year=1852|author={{w|Charles Dickens}}|title=[[s:Bleak House|Bleak House]]|chapter=55|passage=I strongly suspect (from what Small has dropped, and from what we have '''corkscrewed''' out of him) that those letters I was to have brought to your ladyship were not destroyed when I supposed they were.}}
#* '''1922''', [[w:James Thomas Heflin|James Thomas Heflin]], in ''Hearings Before the Committee on Agriculture and Forestry''<sup>[http://books.google.com/books?id=lFYQAAAAIAAJ]</sup>, page 460:
#*: Yes, I believe you did after it was '''corkscrewed''' out of you, but I got the impression at the outset that you were, just as willing to let it stand there.

